---
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { t, localePath } from '../lib/i18n.js';

const locale = 'en';

const entries = (await getCollection('principles', (e) => e.id.startsWith('en/'))).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order
);

const principles = await Promise.all(
  entries.map(async (entry) => {
    const { Content } = await render(entry);
    return { data: entry.data, Content };
  })
);

const lede =
  'Seven principles, each one word and a claim in the imperative, so they survive being quoted in a board meeting. Two of them exist in no other AI framework anywhere, and those two are why this is not a link to the K-12 guidance.';
const description =
  'Seven principles for the use of AI in early childhood education: Relationship, Development, Standing, Custody, Consequence, Language and Currency.';
---
<BaseLayout title="Principles" description={description} locale={locale}>
  <PageHeader
    caption={t(locale, 'nav.principles')}
    title="Seven principles"
    lede={lede}
    locale={locale}
    lastUpdated="2026-09-10"
  />

  <p class="govuk-body">
    The two are these. <a class="govuk-link" href="#development">Development</a>
    sets a developmental-appropriateness test, asking whether
    a tool suits what a child of this age actually needs — a test no framework
    written for K-12 schooling or for generic nonprofits contains.
    <a class="govuk-link" href="#standing">Standing</a> asks who decides for a
    child who cannot notice the system, object to it, recognize it as
    non-human, or report harm from it. Everything else here follows from taking
    those two questions seriously in a room of one-, two- and three-year-olds.
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    The principles do not change across provider size. A family child care home
    and a twelve-site operator are held to the same seven. What changes is the
    paperwork each one needs to meet them, which is what
    <a class="govuk-link" href={localePath(locale, '/policies')}>the policies to adopt</a>
    are for.
  </p>

  <h2 class="govuk-heading-s govuk-!-margin-top-6" id="contents">
    {t(locale, 'label.onThisPage')}
  </h2>
  <ul class="govuk-list">
    {principles.map(({ data }) => (
      <li>
        <a class="govuk-link" href={`#${data.id}`}>
          {data.order}. {data.name}
        </a>{' '}
        &mdash; {data.claim}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  {principles.map(({ data, Content }) => (
    <section id={data.id} class="govuk-!-margin-top-8">
      <hr class="govuk-section-break govuk-section-break--visible govuk-!-margin-bottom-6" />
      <span class="govuk-caption-m">Principle {data.order} of {principles.length}</span>
      <h2 class="govuk-heading-m govuk-!-margin-bottom-2">{data.name}</h2>
      <p class="govuk-body-l"><strong>{data.claim}</strong></p>
      <div class="govuk-body app-principle-body">
        <Content />
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <hr class="govuk-section-break govuk-section-break--visible govuk-!-margin-top-8 govuk-!-margin-bottom-6" />

  <h2 class="govuk-heading-m" id="open-question">An open question: should there be six?</h2>
  <div class="govuk-inset-text">
    <p class="govuk-body">
      The working draft offers <a class="govuk-link" href="#currency">Currency</a>
      as the one to demote if seven principles feel like too many to hold. It
      governs the framework itself rather than the use of AI with children, and
      it could live as a masthead commitment instead of a principle. Six may be
      the sweet spot.
    </p>
    <p class="govuk-body govuk-!-margin-bottom-0">
      This is not settled, and it is recorded here rather than decided quietly.
      If you are using these principles in your own program and have a view,
      that is exactly the kind of feedback this draft is asking for.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
